WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:05.246
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Emma. Welcome to Online C two, Book one.

2
00:00:05.546 --> 00:00:08.862
Hi! I'm Visal. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:09.162 --> 00:00:16.633
Unit six: Cross-Cultural Diplomacy. Today you will describe diplomatic processes with formal passive constructions.

4
00:00:16.933 --> 00:00:19.562
Here is our lesson map. Let's start!

5
00:00:20.500 --> 00:00:23.582
Part one: words. You will learn ten new words.

6
00:00:24.417 --> 00:00:25.963
Cultural competence.

7
00:00:26.950 --> 00:00:31.759
Cultural competence means the ability to interact effectively across cultures.

8
00:00:32.350 --> 00:00:36.114
Cultural competence prevented a minor gesture from becoming an insult.

9
00:00:36.717 --> 00:00:39.857
Your turn. Say: Cultural competence.

10
00:00:42.700 --> 00:00:44.189
Cultural competence.

11
00:00:44.950 --> 00:00:46.497
Diplomatic protocol.

12
00:00:47.483 --> 00:00:52.084
Diplomatic protocol means the formal rules of etiquette in international relations.

13
00:00:52.683 --> 00:00:56.552
Diplomatic protocol dictates the seating order at state dinners.

14
00:00:57.150 --> 00:00:59.721
Your turn. Say: Diplomatic protocol.

15
00:01:02.567 --> 00:01:04.369
Diplomatic protocol.

16
00:01:05.117 --> 00:01:06.756
Negotiated settlement.

17
00:01:07.750 --> 00:01:12.571
Negotiated settlement means an agreement reached through negotiation rather than force.

18
00:01:13.183 --> 00:01:16.042
The border dispute ended in a negotiated settlement.

19
00:01:16.650 --> 00:01:19.999
Your turn. Say: Negotiated settlement.

20
00:01:22.833 --> 00:01:24.543
Negotiated settlement.

21
00:01:25.283 --> 00:01:26.470
Geopolitics.

22
00:01:27.483 --> 00:01:31.909
Geopolitics means politics shaped by geography and power between states.

23
00:01:32.517 --> 00:01:36.861
Mekong geopolitics involves dams, water and great-power rivalry.

24
00:01:37.450 --> 00:01:40.741
Your turn. Say: Geopolitics.

25
00:01:43.600 --> 00:01:44.868
Geopolitics.

26
00:01:45.617 --> 00:01:46.920
Multilateralism.

27
00:01:47.917 --> 00:01:52.807
Multilateralism means cooperation among many countries through shared institutions.

28
00:01:53.417 --> 00:01:56.055
ASEAN embodies regional multilateralism.

29
00:01:56.650 --> 00:01:59.639
Your turn. Say: Multilateralism.

30
00:02:02.500 --> 00:02:03.989
Multilateralism.

31
00:02:04.750 --> 00:02:06.123
Soft power.

32
00:02:07.117 --> 00:02:11.461
Soft power means influence based on attraction rather than coercion.

33
00:02:12.050 --> 00:02:15.385
K-pop has greatly expanded Korea's soft power.

34
00:02:15.983 --> 00:02:19.030
Your turn. Say: Soft power.

35
00:02:21.867 --> 00:02:23.123
Soft power.

36
00:02:23.883 --> 00:02:25.500
Bilateral relations.

37
00:02:26.483 --> 00:02:29.969
Bilateral relations means relations between two countries.

38
00:02:30.583 --> 00:02:33.396
Bilateral relations improved after the trade agreement.

39
00:02:33.983 --> 00:02:37.054
Your turn. Say: Bilateral relations.

40
00:02:39.900 --> 00:02:41.609
Bilateral relations.

41
00:02:42.350 --> 00:02:43.653
Consensus-building.

42
00:02:44.650 --> 00:02:48.182
Consensus-building means the process of reaching general agreement.

43
00:02:48.783 --> 00:02:52.257
Consensus-building is slow but produces durable decisions.

44
00:02:52.850 --> 00:02:55.978
Your turn. Say: Consensus-building.

45
00:02:58.833 --> 00:03:00.183
Consensus-building.

46
00:03:00.917 --> 00:03:01.999
Realpolitik.

47
00:03:02.983 --> 00:03:07.607
Realpolitik means politics based on practical interests rather than ideals.

48
00:03:08.217 --> 00:03:12.225
Realpolitik led both nations to cooperate despite their differences.

49
00:03:12.817 --> 00:03:15.724
Your turn. Say: Realpolitik.

50
00:03:18.567 --> 00:03:19.858
Realpolitik.

51
00:03:20.617 --> 00:03:21.908
Multilateral.

52
00:03:22.917 --> 00:03:26.124
Multilateral means involving three or more countries.

53
00:03:26.717 --> 00:03:29.784
A multilateral agreement on fishing protected the Mekong.

54
00:03:30.383 --> 00:03:33.465
Your turn. Say: Multilateral.

55
00:03:36.300 --> 00:03:37.464
Multilateral.

56
00:03:38.267 --> 00:03:41.720
Part two: dialogue. Let's listen to a conversation.

57
00:03:42.583 --> 00:03:50.588
Visal, the Paris Peace Agreements of 1991 ended Cambodia's long conflict. How was such a complex deal achieved?

58
00:03:51.683 --> 00:03:59.999
It was negotiated over several years, and crucially, it was guaranteed by multiple countries rather than by a single power.

59
00:04:01.083 --> 00:04:02.340
Why did that matter?

60
00:04:03.450 --> 00:04:12.964
Because trust was lacking between the factions. A multilateral framework was established so that no party could be dominated by another.

61
00:04:14.050 --> 00:04:16.154
Were cultural differences an obstacle?

62
00:04:17.250 --> 00:04:25.661
Constantly. Face had to be preserved on all sides, so agreements were often reached privately before being announced publicly.

63
00:04:26.800 --> 00:04:28.753
Part three: grammar.

64
00:04:29.617 --> 00:04:33.114
Grammar focus: Formal passive constructions.

65
00:04:33.414 --> 00:04:34.624
Look at the pattern.

66
00:04:35.317 --> 00:04:38.350
A settlement was negotiated through careful diplomacy.

67
00:04:39.250 --> 00:04:42.132
The framework was guaranteed by multiple countries.

68
00:04:43.017 --> 00:04:46.061
Agreements were reached privately before being announced.

69
00:04:47.000 --> 00:04:58.081
Formal passives foreground outcomes and depersonalise sensitive actions — vital in diplomacy, where blame must be avoided. Note the passive gerund 'before being announced' and the passive perfect 'had been agreed'.

70
00:04:58.817 --> 00:05:02.758
Be careful! Don't say: The deal was agreed before announced.

71
00:05:04.700 --> 00:05:07.512
Say: The deal was agreed before being announced.

72
00:05:07.812 --> 00:05:10.056
Before + being + p.p.

73
00:05:10.867 --> 00:05:13.519
Part four: reading. Read and listen.

74
00:05:14.333 --> 00:05:16.379
Saving Face, Building Peace.

75
00:05:16.679 --> 00:05:24.809
Cross-cultural diplomacy requires an understanding not only of national interests but also of the values through which those interests are expressed.

76
00:05:25.800 --> 00:05:33.488
In many Asian contexts, direct refusal is considered damaging to face, so disagreements are frequently conveyed through indirect language.

77
00:05:34.500 --> 00:05:41.852
The Paris Peace Agreements on Cambodia were negotiated through years of patient consensus-building and were guaranteed by a multilateral framework.

78
00:05:42.867 --> 00:05:50.311
Soft power also shapes diplomacy, since nations admired for their culture find it easier to win cooperation without coercion.

79
00:05:51.300 --> 00:05:57.166
Yet idealism is always tempered by realpolitik, as states ultimately pursue security and prosperity.

80
00:05:58.267 --> 00:06:02.210
Part five: quiz. Five questions. Are you ready?

81
00:06:03.050 --> 00:06:07.035
The treaty blank by twelve nations in 1991.

82
00:06:07.335 --> 00:06:12.207
A, signed. B, was signed. C, has signing.

83
00:06:15.767 --> 00:06:17.778
The answer is B: was signed.

84
00:06:18.917 --> 00:06:22.867
Influence based on attraction rather than coercion is blank.

85
00:06:23.167 --> 00:06:28.074
A, soft power. B, realpolitik. C, geopolitics.

86
00:06:31.633 --> 00:06:33.842
The answer is A: soft power.

87
00:06:34.983 --> 00:06:37.900
Relations between two countries are blank relations.

88
00:06:38.200 --> 00:06:42.364
A, multilateral. B, consensus. C, bilateral.

89
00:06:45.900 --> 00:06:47.876
The answer is C: bilateral.

90
00:06:49.017 --> 00:06:50.691
Choose the correct sentence.

91
00:06:50.991 --> 00:06:58.627
A, The deal was agreed before announced.. B, The deal was agreed before being announced.. C, The deal agreed before being announced..

92
00:07:02.167 --> 00:07:05.803
The answer is B: The deal was agreed before being announced..

93
00:07:06.950 --> 00:07:11.167
According to the reading, the Paris Peace Agreements were guaranteed by…

94
00:07:11.467 --> 00:07:16.676
A, one superpower. B, a multilateral framework. C, Cambodia alone.

95
00:07:20.233 --> 00:07:23.208
The answer is B: a multilateral framework.

96
00:07:24.400 --> 00:07:27.412
Part six: speaking. It's your turn to talk.

97
00:07:28.250 --> 00:07:31.840
Why is cultural competence important in international diplomacy?

98
00:07:48.650 --> 00:07:50.197
Now listen to Visal.

99
00:07:50.497 --> 00:08:28.182
Cultural competence is essential because diplomatic messages are interpreted through cultural expectations as much as through words. A proposal that seems efficient in one culture may be perceived as arrogant in another, and negotiations can collapse over issues of respect rather than substance. For example, in many Asian contexts, a public refusal causes loss of face, so skilled diplomats signal disagreement indirectly. Without such awareness, even well-intentioned agreements may never be reached.

100
00:08:28.482 --> 00:08:30.191
Excellent answer, Visal!

101
00:08:31.083 --> 00:08:35.953
Great work! Now you can use ten new words and formal passive constructions.

102
00:08:36.253 --> 00:08:41.996
Today's value is global citizenship. We all share one world. Help people near and far.

103
00:08:42.296 --> 00:08:44.705
See you in Unit seven. Goodbye!

104
00:08:45.005 --> 00:08:47.425
Thank you, Ms. Emma! Bye, everyone!

